<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-outcomes-week-10-11">
  <title>Week 10 and Week 11</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Outcome E5 was introduced in Week 8 and Week 9, and it will be repeated this block.
      You will have another opportunity to demonstrate it alongside the new outcomes.
    </p>
    <p>
      These weeks focus on evaluating indeterminate limits with L'Hôpital's Rule,
      applying the second part of the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus to
      definite integrals whose antiderivatives involve exponentials and logarithms,
      and building right triangle models to solve applied problems with trigonometry.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-E5-repeat">
    <title>E5 Logarithmic Differentiation (Repeated)</title>

    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-E5-repeat">
      <title>E5</title>
      <p>
        I can use logarithmic differentiation on <m>y=f(x)</m> by differentiating <m>\ln(|y|)</m>
        with the chain rule to solve for <m>y' = f'(x)</m> after taking advantage of the expanded logarithm.
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <remark>
      <p>
        This outcome is unchanged from Week 8 and Week 9.
        See <xref ref="subsec-E5" text="title"/> for the checkpoint practice problem.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-E6">
    <title>E6 L'Hôpital's Rule</title>

    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-E6">
      <title>E6</title>
      <p>
        I can apply L'Hôpital's rule for indeterminate limits of the form <m>0/0</m> or
        <m>\infty/\infty</m>, including rewriting other indeterminate forms so that
        L'Hôpital's rule can be used.
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <exercise label="E6_LHopitals_Rule">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the limit <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x\to 0} \frac{e^{-3x} - 1 + 3x}{x^2}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-I3">
    <title>I3 Fundamental Theorem of Calculus Part 2 with Exponentials</title>

    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-I3">
      <title>I3</title>
      <p>
        I can apply the second part of the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus to calculate
        definite integrals of continuous functions by identifying antiderivatives involving
        exponentials and then evaluating its change.
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <exercise label="I3_FTC2_Exponentials">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Compute <m>\displaystyle \int_{-1}^{2} x e^{-x^2} \, dx</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We will use the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus to evaluate the definite integral,
          which involves identifying an antiderivative of the integrand <m>x e^{-x^2}</m>
          and then computing its change.
          When exponentials are involved, we first check if the integrand is compatible with
          the derivative of <m>e^u</m>, which is <m>e^{u} u'</m>.
          In this case <m>u=-x^2</m> so <m>u'=-2x</m>.
          The form <m>e^u u' = -2x e^{-x^2}</m> is close to the integrand <m>x e^{-x^2}</m>,
          but we need to multiply and divide by <m>-2</m> to get that form.
        </p>
        <md>
          <mrow>\int_{-1}^{2} x e^{-x^2} \, dx \amp = \int_{-1}^{2} \frac{-2x e^{-x^2}}{-2} \, dx</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp \overset{\text{FTC}}{=} \left[ \frac{-1}{2} e^{-x^2} \right]_{-1}^{2}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp = \left( \frac{-1}{2} e^{-4} \right) - \left( \frac{-1}{2} e^{-1} \right)</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp = \frac{e^{-1} - e^{-4}}{2}</mrow>
        </md>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-I4">
    <title>I4 Fundamental Theorem of Calculus Part 2 with Logarithms</title>

    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-I4">
      <title>I4</title>
      <p>
        I can apply the second part of the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus to calculate
        definite integrals of continuous functions by identifying antiderivatives involving
        logarithms and then evaluating its change.
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <exercise label="I4_FTC2_Logarithms">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Compute <m>\displaystyle \int_0^2 \frac{4x}{x^2+1} \, dx</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We will use the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus to evaluate the definite integral,
          which involves identifying an antiderivative of the integrand
          <m>\displaystyle \frac{4x}{x^2+1}</m> and then computing its change.
          When logarithms are involved, we look for an integrand with the structure
          <m>u'/u</m>, because that matches the derivative of <m>\ln(|u|)</m>.
          In this case the denominator suggests <m>u=x^2+1</m>, so <m>u'=2x</m>.
          The form <m>u'/u = \dfrac{2x}{x^2+1}</m> is close to the integrand,
          but the numerator <m>4x</m> is twice as large, so we need to factor out a <m>2</m>
          to get that form.
          Since <m>x^2+1</m> is always positive, the absolute value does not change anything on this interval.
        </p>
        <md>
          <mrow>\int_0^2 \frac{4x}{x^2+1} \, dx \amp = \int_0^2 2 \cdot \frac{2x}{x^2+1} \, dx</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp \overset{\text{FTC}}{=} \Big[ 2 \ln\left(\left|x^2+1\right|\right) \Big]_0^2</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp = 2 \ln(5) - 2 \ln(1)</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp = 2 \ln(5)</mrow>
        </md>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-T1">
    <title>T1 Right Triangle Trigonometry</title>

    <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-T1">
      <title>T1</title>
      <p>
        I can create a right triangle model for an applied problem involving lengths and angles
        and use basic trigonometric ratios to compute the length of an unmeasured side.
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <exercise label="T1_Right_Triangle_Trigonometry">
      <statement>
        <p>
          You are standing on one side of a river along a section where the river is exactly
          20 feet wide and flows in a perfectly straight path.
          Your friend is on the opposite bank, but you have to turn 50 degrees to the right
          to be looking directly at them.
          Use a triangle model and trigonometry to determine the distance between you.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          To create a triangle model, draw two parallel lines to represent the banks of the river.
          Draw a line straight across the river to represent your position and the position
          directly across from you on the other bank.
          Draw a second line from your position, rotated to the right by <m>50^\circ</m>,
          to represent the distance to your friend.
          Label the angle, label the distance across the river as <m>20</m> feet,
          and label the unknown distance between you and your friend with a variable, say <m>D</m>.
          Connecting your friend's position to the point directly across from you
          completes a right triangle.
        </p>
        <image width="50%">
          <shortdescription>
            Two horizontal parallel lines represent the river banks, with the river shaded between them.
            You stand at a point on the lower bank. A vertical segment of length 20 feet goes straight up
            to the point directly across on the upper bank, with a right angle marked there.
            A second segment of length D goes from you up and to the right to your friend on the upper bank.
            The angle between the two segments at your position is marked as 50 degrees.
          </shortdescription>
          <latex-image>
            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.2]
              \fill[blue!10] (-1.5,0) rectangle (5,3);
              \draw[thick] (-1.5,0) -- (5,0);
              \draw[thick] (-1.5,3) -- (5,3);
              \draw[thick] (0,0) -- (0,3);
              \draw[thick] (0,0) -- (3.575,3);
              \draw[thick] (0,3) -- (3.575,3);
              \draw (0,2.8) -- (0.2,2.8) -- (0.2,3);
              \draw (0,0.9) arc (90:40:0.9);
              \node at (0.55,1.2) {$50^\circ$};
              \node[left] at (0,1.5) {$20$ ft};
              \node[below right] at (1.8,1.5) {$D$};
              \fill (0,0) circle (2pt);
              \fill (3.575,3) circle (2pt);
              \node[below] at (0,0) {You};
              \node[above] at (3.575,3) {Friend};
              \node[right] at (5,1.5) {River};
            \end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
        </image>
        <p>
          The given angle of <m>50^\circ</m> has the known length (the width of the river)
          as its adjacent leg, and the unknown length (the distance to your friend)
          is the hypotenuse.
          This tells us to use the cosine ratio of the adjacent length to the hypotenuse length.
        </p>
        <md>
          <mrow>\cos(50^\circ) \amp = \frac{20}{D}</mrow>
          <mrow>D \cos(50^\circ) \amp = 20</mrow>
          <mrow>D \amp = \frac{20}{\cos(50^\circ)}</mrow>
          <mrow>D \amp \approx 31.1 \text{ ft}</mrow>
        </md>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

</section>
